In my metadata spreadsheet, I chose to record page orientation and content type for each scanned page of the diary.  \
I made this decision because these two fields highlight both the material and narrative variation within the text.  \
The orientation (horizontal, vertical, or mixed) captures how Hadden used the physical space of the page, which helps reveal shifts between formal diary entries, sketches, and scattered notes.  \
The content type distinguishes whether each page contains continuous writing, drawings, or blank space, allowing patterns in the diary\'92s structure and composition to emerge across the digital facsimile.  \
\
I focused on these categories rather than broader bibliographic data because they offer insights that would be most useful for digital editions and research datasets: they connect the visual layout of each image to the literary and personal content of the diary.  \
Given the diversity of the pages, from prose to landscape sketches to vocabulary lists, these metadata choices help future users navigate the document not only by page number but by type of visual and textual experience.\
\
\
For the extracting & cleaning text assignment, I began by using Transkribus to extract the text from my scanned manuscript. However, the accuracy was extremely poor; almost every word was incorrect, and the output consisted almost entirely of nonsensical strings of letters that bore no resemblance to the original writing. Because of this, I decided to switch to ChatGPT for text recognition. The results were noticeably more coherent, producing sentences that generally made sense contextually, but many words were still inaccurate or replaced with guesses based on deduction. As a result, I had to manually go through each page and make extensive edits to bring the text closer to the original.
Throughout the process, I observed several recurring challenges. The accuracy dropped significantly on pages that contained writing in multiple orientations, such as when text was written vertically or diagonally. The model also struggled with numbers, often misreading or omitting them altogether. Additionally, some sections of the original text were very faint because they appeared to have been written in pencil. These lighter areas often caused the extraction tools to skip entire sentences or paragraphs, leading to noticeable gaps in the output.
The overall process of correction was slow and detail-oriented, as the handwriting in my source material was cursive and often difficult to interpret, even manually. Despite these difficulties, I was impressed by how well ChatGPT was able to capture the general structure and meaning of the text given the complexity of the handwriting. The experience highlighted how challenging digitization can be when dealing with handwritten and low-contrast materials, and how much human correction and interpretation are still required to make the text usable. This process gave me a deeper appreciation for the amount of labor and time that goes into cleaning and correcting scanned historical materials and the importance of documenting the limitations and interpretive choices made along the way.

